/**
 * Wave 4, family 6 (24 Sept 2026): rare tumour entities from the public list and the corpus's subtypes arrays
 * (docs/CANCER-PAGES.md): adamantinoma of bone; dedifferentiated and poorly differentiated chordoma; the pineal region
 * tumours (pineocytoma with pineal parenchymal tumour of intermediate differentiation, pineoblastoma, papillary tumour
 * of the pineal region); choroid plexus carcinoma; optic pathway glioma; pleuropulmonary blastoma proper (types I, Ir,
 * II and III under the childhood lung tumour umbrella page); the functioning pancreatic neuroendocrine tumours
 * glucagonoma, VIPoma and somatostatinoma; conjunctival melanoma; and desmoplastic small round cell tumour.
 *
 * Rule cases: "chondroid chordoma" and "conventional chordoma" are the parent page (conventional is the chordoma the
 * page describes; chondroid is a pattern within it in the WHO bone classification), so they are aliases, not pages.
 * "Pineal astrocytoma" is an astrocytoma by site and belongs to the glioma pages; "primitive neuroectodermal tumour"
 * and "myxosarcoma" are retired terms; "fibrocartilaginous mesenchymoma" has too little published material for a
 * sourced page. Ganglioneuroma, neurofibroma, adrenocortical adenoma and haemangioblastoma are benign and are not
 * cancer records; keratoacanthoma is a variant of well-differentiated cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma in the WHO skin
 * classification and stays a string. "Islet cell carcinoma" is the pancreatic neuroendocrine tumour page; "Kidney
 * cancer", "ovarian epithelial cancer" and "serous tumour" are existing pages. Epithelioid haemangioendothelioma already
 * had a page. Every figure is quoted from the paper or page named beside it. Registered in src/data/index.ts as
 * cancersWave4Rare.
 */
